``` Introduction

`ore/html.ore` isn't part of the default preload -- it needs an explicit `@load`. It declares one generic `Dom` type plus a predefined element for each common tag (`Div`, `P`, `H1`, `A`, ...), each just `Dom` composed with its own `html_element` tag name. Calling `.to_s()` renders an element (and its `children`) straight to an HTML string.
```
@load 'ore/html.ore'

sign := Div([P('Welcome to the beach')])
@puts "rendered sign: `sign.to_s()`"   # rendered sign: <div><p>Welcome to the beach</p></div>


``` Nesting and render{;}

A `Dom` type's `render{;}` returns what belongs inside its tag -- by default that's just `children`, but overriding it lets a type build its own inner structure instead of taking children at all.
```
Camp_Sign | Dom {
	message,

	new { message;
		./message = message
	}

	render {; P(message) }
}

@puts "rendered camp sign: `Camp_Sign('Do not drink the water').to_s()`"   # rendered camp sign: <div><p>Do not drink the water</p></div>


``` html_* and css_* prefixes

An `html_*` declaration becomes an HTML attribute (`html_class` -> `class`); a `css_*` declaration becomes an inline style property (`css_padding` -> `padding`). Both are just regular declarations underneath, readable like any other -- but they're only actually folded into attribute/style output by the server's own rendering pipeline when a `Dom` instance comes back from a route (see learn/web_server.ore). Calling `.to_s()` directly, like above, ignores them entirely and only wraps `children` in the tag.
```
Card | Div {
	css_padding := '1rem'
	css_background_color := '#f5deb3'
	html_class := 'card'
}

card := Card(P('Found supplies'))
@puts "card.css_padding: `card.css_padding`"   # card.css_padding: 1rem -- a plain declaration, readable directly
@puts "card.html_class: `card.html_class`"     # card.html_class: card
@puts "rendered card: `card.to_s()`"           # rendered card: <div><p>Found supplies</p></div> -- no class or style attribute here
